---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base title="Using ERA" description="What you can take from ERA today, and what is not ready.">
  <h1>Using ERA</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    There are three levels of using a design system, and being honest about which ones work matters more than claiming all
    three. Two of them work today. The third does not yet, and the reason is worth reading before you plan around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Level 1: read it</h2>

  <p>
    Everything is public. The <a href={`${base}/storybook/`}>Storybook</a> has every component with every state, the
    <a href="https://github.com/DARKHACK4949/Era-Design-Foundation-Story-book">repository</a> has the source, and this site
    has the reasoning. If you are building your own system and want to see how someone else handled multi-brand theming or
    where the contrast traps are, that is all here and you do not need anything from this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Level 2: take the tokens</h2>

  <p>
    This works today and it is the part most worth taking. <code>tokens/generated/era.css</code> is plain CSS custom
    properties with no dependencies, no build step and no framework. Drop it in, set two attributes, and 1,505 variables are
    available:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`<link rel="stylesheet" href="era.css">
<html data-brand="brand-2" data-theme="dark">

/* then, anywhere */
.my-button {
  background: var(--sem-primary-bg);
  color: var(--sem-primary-fg-on-bg);
  border-radius: var(--brand-radius-roles-buttons-button-md);
  padding-inline: var(--spacing-space-16);
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    It works with React, Vue, Svelte, plain HTML, or as the theme layer under Tailwind. There is also
    <code>tokens/generated/tokens.json</code> in W3C design-token format, with every brand and mode under
    <code>$extensions</code>, if you would rather run it through Style Dictionary and emit your own platform targets.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both files are generated from the Figma library by <code>npm run tokens</code>, and the build fails on any reference that
    does not resolve, so what you get cannot have drifted from the design source.
  </p>

  <h2>Level 3: install the components</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>This does not work yet, and that is a deliberate choice rather than an oversight.</strong>
    The package is marked private and declares no entry points, so <code>npm install</code> of this repository gets you
    nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Publishing a component library is a maintenance commitment: a versioning policy, a deprecation policy, a changelog people
    can rely on, and an answer every time someone needs a prop that does not exist. ERA is a personal system. Taking on that
    commitment and then not meeting it would be worse than not making the promise.
  </p>

  <p>So the supported path is the one shadcn made normal: copy what you need.</p>

  <pre><code>{`# the tokens
cp tokens/generated/era.css your-project/src/styles/

# a component, with its stylesheet
cp -r src/components/button your-project/src/components/
cp src/lib/cn.ts your-project/src/lib/`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Each component folder is self-contained: one <code>.tsx</code>, one <code>.css</code>, and a single import of
    <code>cn</code>. There is no runtime dependency, no context provider to wire up and no theme object to pass around.
    Once <code>era.css</code> is loaded and <code>data-brand</code> and <code>data-theme</code> are set, the component is
    already themed.
  </p>

  <p>
    The trade-off of copying is the obvious one: you own what you copied and you will not get fixes automatically. For a
    system this size that is usually the better deal, because the thing you most want to change in someone else's component
    is the part a package would stop you from touching.
  </p>

  <h2>If you want the Figma side too</h2>

  <p>
    The tokens here are exported from a Figma library with the same three collections, the same names and the same modes. A
    designer working in Figma and an engineer working from <code>era.css</code> are reading the same token names, which is
    the actual point of doing it this way.
  </p>

  <h2>Licence</h2>

  <p>
    MIT. Use it, change it, ship it commercially, no attribution required beyond keeping the licence notice. If you do build
    something on it, an issue saying so would be nice but is not owed.
  </p>
</Base>
